(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-exam-2-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which delivery route carries the risk of globe perforation, optic nerve trauma and central retinal artery occlusion?","choices":["Topical instillation","Oral administration","Periocular injection","Intravenous administration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These periocular injections reach the anterior segment, posterior uveitis and cystoid macular edema, but the needle is the hazard — perforation, optic nerve trauma, vascular occlusion and direct retinal drug toxicity if the globe is entered. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Statins, ezetimibe, bile acid sequestrants and PCSK9 inhibitors lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) through which shared end result?","choices":["Less VLDL secreted by the liver","More active hepatic LDL receptors","More HDL made by the liver","Less bile acid in the intestine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Statins and resins upregulate them, ezetimibe raises their expression by cutting cholesterol supply, and PCSK9 inhibitors keep them active longer. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is a patient on aspirin told not to take it with an anticoagulant?","choices":["The anticoagulant blocks aspirin's absorption","Aspirin makes the anticoagulant ineffective","The combination causes Reye's syndrome","Aspirin inhibits platelet function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two different parts of hemostasis are disabled at once, which is more than the sum of either alone. Adding a second bleeding mechanism. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why is niacin a relative contraindication in a patient with symptomatic gout?","choices":["It causes gallstones","It raises potassium","It raises uric acid","It lowers kidney blood flow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At larger doses niacin raises liver enzymes, glucose and uric acid, so gout and significant hyperuricemia are relative contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A newborn needs prophylaxis against ophthalmia neonatorum. Which agent?","choices":["Erythromycin ointment","Azithromycin solution","Moxifloxacin solution","Natamycin suspension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the named agent for newborn prophylaxis, and it is inexpensive. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient starts an ocular antihistamine. How long before they feel relief, and how long before its full effect can be judged?","choices":["Five to fourteen days before any effect at all","Minutes for relief, two weeks for full efficacy","Full effect immediately, with no further change","Six weeks, as with most topical ocular therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter in counseling — the drug does something immediately, so it will not feel useless, but the final verdict takes a fortnight. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"By how much does high-intensity statin therapy lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["By 30 to 49%","By less than 30%","By 50% or more","By 10 to 20%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High intensity lowers LDL by 50% or more; moderate intensity by 30 to 49%; low intensity by less than 30%. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which condition is an absolute contraindication to bile acid sequestrants?","choices":["Pregnancy in the third trimester","Childhood or adolescence","Chronic hepatic disease","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia (with its increased triglycerides) and triglycerides above 400 mg/dL are absolute contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient develops a persistent dry cough six weeks after starting lisinopril. What is the likely cause?","choices":["Angiotensin II acting on the airways","Bradykinin accumulating in the lungs","Aldosterone excess retaining fluid","Beta-2 blockade in the bronchi"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin and substance P accumulate because the converting enzyme no longer breaks them down; the cough appears between 1 week and 6 months. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which agent is described as the most common ophthalmic antibiotic, and what property lets it be used before a bacterial cause is confirmed?","choices":["Azithromycin, because it is dosed less often","Erythromycin, because it is soothing on an inflamed eye","Ciprofloxacin, because it covers the widest spectrum","Bacitracin, because it has no drug interactions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the one you will see most, and because it soothes the inflamed eye it can reasonably be started even when a bacterial infection has not been confirmed. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What effect does clonidine have on blood glucose?","choices":["It lowers it","It raises it","It masks lows only","It has no effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine increases blood glucose by inhibiting insulin secretion. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"When are cultures routinely taken before starting an ophthalmic antibiotic?","choices":["Always, because resistance is emerging","Only for conjunctivitis, not for keratitis","Only when the patient wears contact lenses","Not unless an unusual organism is expected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum topical therapy is started empirically. Cultures are reserved for when something unusual is likely — the immunocompromised patient is the example given. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Hydralazine is N-acetylated in the liver. Which patients are at higher risk of its lupus syndrome?","choices":["Fast acetylators","Patients on low doses","Patients on short courses","Slow acetylators"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Slow acetylators clear hydralazine more slowly; with high doses, long-term use, female sex and Caucasian ancestry, this raises the risk of lupus syndrome. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which vessels does nitroprusside dilate?","choices":["Veins and arterioles","Arterioles only","Veins only","Coronary arteries only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitric oxide released from nitroprusside activates cyclic GMP and lowers intracellular calcium in both veins and arterioles. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A patient planning a pregnancy needs LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering. Which class is approved in pregnancy?","choices":["Statins","Bile acid sequestrants","Fibrates","Statin with niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins are not absorbed and are approved in pregnancy, whereas pregnancy is a listed contraindication for statins and for fibrates. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How should a patient prepare cholestyramine powder?","choices":["Swallow the powder dry","Dissolve it under the tongue","Mix it with an antacid","Mix it in water or fruit juice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Powders must be mixed with water or fruit juice, and a pulpy drink masks the taste. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A drop instilled in the eye reaches the systemic circulation and causes a side effect elsewhere in the body. By which route did it get there, and what is the consequence for its metabolism?","choices":["Transcorneal absorption, which avoids first-pass metabolism","Nasolacrimal drainage, with extensive first-pass metabolism","Conjunctival absorption into the retinal circulation","Nasolacrimal drainage, which avoids first-pass metabolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Drug draining through the nasolacrimal duct is absorbed across nasal mucosa straight into the systemic circulation, bypassing the liver's first pass. That is why a topical drop can produce a systemic effect out of proportion to its dose. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which single-dose option is offered for streptococcal pharyngitis in a patient unlikely to finish a course?","choices":["A ten-day course of oral azithromycin","A ten-day course of oral amoxicillin","Benzathine penicillin G by intramuscular injection","Intramuscular ceftriaxone given over three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One injection replaces the whole oral course, which is precisely its point when adherence is in doubt. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"At follow-up, a 61-year-old's documented visual acuity is worse than at the first visit. What does that require?","choices":["Repeating the course of drops","Reassurance and review in a week","Immediate ophthalmology consult","Switching to an ointment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is the reason acuity is documented at every visit. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Beyond ocular irritation, which adverse effect is specific to trifluridine?","choices":["A white corneal precipitate","Raised intraocular pressure","Changes in iris color","Punctate keratopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is worth separating from the generic irritation every ophthalmic drug can cause, because this one names a drug and an actual corneal finding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient on verapamil needs a statin. Which statin would CYP3A4 inhibition affect least?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Simvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rosuvastatin has minimal CYP metabolism, so verapamil's CYP3A4 inhibition matters least; atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are CYP3A4 substrates. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How do tetracaine and proparacaine work?","choices":["They inhibit sodium influx into the neuron","They block muscarinic receptors on the ciliary muscle","They activate alpha-1 receptors to vasoconstrict","They inhibit prostaglandin synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the sodium channel stops the action potential, so the nerve cannot signal. It is the same mechanism as any local anesthetic, applied to the ocular surface. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Besides lowering angiotensin II, what does blocking angiotensin-converting enzyme do to bradykinin?","choices":["Speeds its breakdown","Blocks its receptor","Leaves it unchanged","Slows its breakdown"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The same enzyme that makes angiotensin II also inactivates bradykinin, so bradykinin accumulates -- the root of both the dry cough and angioedema. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which description fits alirocumab?","choices":["Oral bile acid-binding resin","Oral HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","Oral cholesterol absorption blocker","Injectable monoclonal antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are injectable-only monoclonal antibodies, and they are expensive. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which statin is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4?","choices":["Simvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are the CYP3A4 statins, so CYP3A4 inhibitors such as verapamil raise their levels. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient takes nadolol. How is it classified?","choices":["Non-selective beta blocker","Beta-1 selective blocker","Alpha-1 and beta blocker","Central alpha-2 agonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nadolol is one of the first-generation, non-selective agents, with propranolol, penbutolol, pindolol, sotalol and timolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with stable angina takes atenolol. His exertional chest pain persists. Pulse is 64 beats per minute, and the electrocardiogram shows normal conduction. Which added drug carries the greatest danger of symptomatic bradycardia and heart block?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Felodipine","Isosorbide mononitrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker also slows the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes, so adding it to a beta blocker risks bradycardia and block. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In thyrotoxicosis, beta blockers relieve tachycardia, tremor and anxiety. What additional effect do they have on thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3)?","choices":["Less thyroid hormone synthesis","More thyroid-stimulating hormone","Less peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion","Less iodine uptake by the gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They reduce the peripheral conversion of thyroxine (T4) to the more active triiodothyronine (T3), besides blunting the adrenergic symptoms. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A patient taking lisinopril and amlodipine remains above goal after titration, and adherence and measurement have been confirmed. What is the next step?","choices":["Add losartan","Add a thiazide-like diuretic","Add hydralazine","Swap amlodipine for verapamil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker plus a dihydropyridine, the next step is a thiazide-like diuretic; if pressure is still uncontrolled, it is apparent resistant hypertension. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has conjunctivitis. What is the advice about resuming lens wear?","choices":["Stop, and resume after 24 hours clear of inflammation","Continue wearing them, since the drops will treat the eye anyway","Stop, and resume as soon as the drops are finished","Stop permanently and switch to spectacles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three parts matter — stop, wait for a clear 24 hours, and deal with the lens itself. Eye makeup should go too, since it is the other reservoir. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 61-year-old with hypertension had a myocardial infarction last year. Which class should generally be used initially?","choices":["An alpha-1 blocker","Hydralazine","Clonidine","A beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A history of myocardial infarction is a reason to start a beta blocker early; beta blockade protects ischemic myocardium and protects against recurrent infarction. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"What happens when ibuprofen is combined with an anticoagulant?","choices":["The anticoagulant is inactivated","Absorption of both drugs is reduced","Clotting time shortens","Prothrombin time is prolonged"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen damages the mucosa and the anticoagulant stops the bleeding from being controlled, which is why the combination is worse than either alone. With a risk of serious gastrointestinal bleeding. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"When is injected ceftriaxone used in acute otitis media?","choices":["For failed therapy","As first line in any child under two","Whenever the tympanic membrane has perforated","Only after culture confirms a resistant organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both routes to it are practical rather than microbiological: the oral agents have not worked, or they cannot be kept down. Or when oral medication is not tolerated. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Besides rebound congestion, what systemic effect is described with oxymetazoline?","choices":["A slow heart rate","Drowsiness and sedation","Raised blood pressure","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A drug sprayed into the nose still reaches the circulation, which is what makes it worth asking about in a hypertensive patient. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A patient taking verapamil is started on a statin. Which statin's level rises most?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Simvastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Simvastatin is a cytochrome P450 3A4 substrate, and verapamil inhibits that enzyme, so simvastatin levels climb (statin metabolism is covered with the lipid-lowering drugs). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus has exertional angina. She has no history of myocardial infarction, heart failure or hypertension, and no asthma. Heart rate is 76 beats per minute and blood pressure is 124/76 mmHg. In the traditional comorbidity-based approach, which class is first line?","choices":["A non-cardioselective beta blocker","A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A short-acting nitrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With diabetes and no other reason for a beta blocker, the traditional table gives a non-dihydropyridine as first line; a long-acting nitrate or a cardioselective beta blocker is the alternative. Current practice is more flexible and still accepts a cardioselective beta blocker first. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How is ezetimibe typically used?","choices":["As first-line monotherapy","For triglycerides over 1000","Added to a statin","As a weekly injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It pairs with a statin for dual inhibition -- the statin blocks synthesis, ezetimibe blocks absorption -- for additional LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why are bile acid sequestrants avoided when triglycerides are high?","choices":["They cause gallstones","They may raise triglycerides","They lower HDL sharply","They damage the liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins may increase VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production, raising triglycerides; on the lipid comparison, resins show no change or a rise in triglycerides. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is a patient on prednisone told about infection risk?","choices":["They should take prophylactic antibiotics","Infection risk only matters above a certain amount","They are immunocompromised and should avoid chickenpox","There is no increased risk with short courses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Live vaccines are the part most easily missed, because the patient does not think of a vaccine as an exposure. Measles and live vaccines. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"In the traditional approach to acute coronary syndrome, how is a beta blocker given?","choices":["Orally only, after discharge","Orally first, then intravenously","Sublingually, then by patch","Intravenously first, then orally"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The traditional sequence is an intravenous beta blocker followed by an oral one. Current guidelines favor oral therapy within the first day and avoid the intravenous route when there is heart failure, low output or a risk of shock. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How do bile acid sequestrants lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["They bind bile acids in the gut","They inhibit HMG-CoA reductase","They block brush-border uptake","They activate PPAR-alpha"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bound bile acids are excreted in feces instead of recirculating, so the liver makes more bile acids from cholesterol and more LDL receptors, lowering LDL. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Through which second messenger pathway do H1 receptors raise intracellular calcium?","choices":["Phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG","Adenylate cyclase, raising cyclic AMP","Direct opening of a ligand-gated sodium channel","Inhibition of guanylate cyclase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the contrast with H2 that matters: H1 works through phospholipase C, H2 through adenylate cyclase and cyclic AMP. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What underlying cellular abnormality is described in Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Autoimmune destruction of hepatocytes","Mitochondrial dysfunction in brain","Direct viral invasion of the brain","Accumulation of unconjugated bilirubin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. One failing organelle across several tissues explains why an illness of the liver and brain also involves muscle. Liver and muscle. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 70-year-old on timolol drops develops fatigue and a heart rate of 48. What has happened?","choices":["An allergic reaction to the preservative","Rebound hyperemia","Systemic beta blockade","Carbonic anhydrase inhibition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Worsening heart failure, bradycardia, heart block and increased airway resistance are all possible from a drop. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What should a patient starting hydralazine be told about stool color?","choices":["Stools may turn orange","Stools may turn pale","Stools may turn black","Stools will not change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stools may turn black on hydralazine, so the patient is warned ahead of time rather than being alarmed by it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Transcorneal absorption has a lag time, and its rate depends most on one variable. Which, and what law governs it?","choices":["The tear film pH, governed by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation","The concentration gradient, governed by Fick's law","The corneal blood flow, governed by Fick's principle","The drug's molecular weight, governed by Graham's law"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transcorneal movement is passive diffusion, so the steeper the gradient the faster the transfer. That is also why raising concentration, rather than volume, is the lever that matters. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the standard of therapy for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate","High-dose amoxicillin alone","Azithromycin","Doxycycline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unlike otitis media, this one starts with the clavulanate already on board, because beta-lactamase producing Haemophilus influenzae is common here. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 22-year-old has watery, red eyes after a family member had the same. Adenoviral conjunctivitis is diagnosed. What is the treatment?","choices":["Trifluridine drops","Oral valacyclovir","Symptomatic relief of irritation","A topical fluoroquinolone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It runs a self-limited course. There is no antiviral for it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which step begins thrombus formation over an atherosclerotic plaque?","choices":["Platelet aggregation at the plaque","Formation of fibrin strands","Occlusion of the lumen by thrombus","Injury to the overlying endothelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spontaneous plaque rupture or procedural injury such as balloon deployment leaves injured endothelium, which starts platelet adhesion and the clotting cascade. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What effect does adding a CYP3A4 inhibitor have on a non-dihydropyridine such as verapamil?","choices":["It lengthens its half-life","It shortens its half-life","It blocks its absorption","It has no effect on it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. CYP3A4 inhibitors increase the half-life of non-dihydropyridines, and of dihydropyridines as well. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Traditionally, when should a patient replace sublingual nitroglycerin tablets after opening the bottle?","choices":["Every 3 to 6 months","Every 1 to 2 weeks","Every 12 to 18 months","Only when the bottle is empty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The traditional counseling is to replace opened tablets every 3 to 6 months, so that a fresh supply is on hand in an emergency. Current labeling instead ties expiry to the printed date when the tablets stay in the tightly closed original glass bottle. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 34-year-old nonsmoker has chest pain that wakes her at 4 a.m., with ST-segment elevation that resolves. Which antianginal class is best avoided?","choices":["Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is vasospastic (variant) angina; beta blockers may worsen symptoms, while calcium channel blockers and nitrates reduce them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A transplant recipient taking tacrolimus is started on diltiazem. What is expected to happen to the tacrolimus level?","choices":["It falls","It is unchanged","It rises","It becomes unmeasurable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, and tacrolimus and cyclosporine are on both lists, so their levels rise. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How does dextromethorphan suppress cough?","choices":["It anesthetizes airway stretch receptors","It constricts bronchial vessels","It breaks disulfide bonds in mucus","It suppresses the medullary cough center"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is related to codeine and acts centrally, which is the origin of both its confusion as a side effect and its misuse. It does so through sigma receptor activation, and it is related to codeine. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which patient must not be given dextromethorphan?","choices":["One taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor","One taking an oral antihistamine","One with treated hypertension","One with a productive, chesty cough"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The two-week tail is the part that catches people out, because the patient has already stopped the drug. The restriction extends for two weeks after stopping one, which is the part most easily missed. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does niacin do to glucose control at larger doses?","choices":["Improves glucose tolerance","Masks hypoglycemia","Decreases glucose tolerance","Has no glucose effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Larger doses raise glucose and decrease glucose tolerance, making diabetes a relative contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Who is affected by Reye's syndrome, and how dangerous is it?","choices":["Adults over sixty-five, with mortality around a tenth","Children, with mortality around half","Children, but it is almost never fatal","Any age, with mortality under one percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The severity is what justifies a blanket restriction on a cheap and otherwise useful drug. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 34-year-old has a dendritic corneal lesion consistent with herpes simplex keratitis. Which TOPICAL agent is available?","choices":["Natamycin","Valacyclovir","Foscarnet","Trifluridine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and its ocular toxicity is punctate keratopathy. Ganciclovir is the other ophthalmic preparation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient with type 1 diabetes is starting propranolol. What should he be told about hypoglycemia?","choices":["It will become less frequent","Warning signs may be masked","Insulin will no longer be needed","Blood sugar will run high only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers inhibit glycogenolysis, so hypoglycemia lasts longer, and they mask its adrenergic warning symptoms. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which agent is singled out as having the strongest antimuscarinic action, especially for motion sickness?","choices":["Doxylamine","Promethazine","Azelastine","Cetirizine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The antimuscarinic strength is what makes it the motion sickness choice, not its antihistamine potency. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
